INT. COASTAL RADIO BOOTH - NIGHT

A cramped shack of salt-warped panels. A single microphone, a mixing board, a kettle. Waves slap the pilings below. A phone rests on the desk.

SAOIRSE TALLCHIEF, 30s, in a fisherman's sweater, sits at the board with her feet on the desk. A "LIVE" lamp glows red. She leans toward the mic.

SAOIRSE
(into mic)
Good evening, Gull Point. It's eleven past midnight, the tide is turning, and this is Saoirse with the Long Wave. Whoever's out there on the water, you're not alone.

She clicks off the mic. She lowers her feet. She rubs her face.

SAOIRSE (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Stop being so cheerful. Nobody likes it.

The phone buzzes. She picks it up.

SAOIRSE (CONT'D)
Long Wave.

DEV (V.O.)
(over phone, warm, unhurried)
Hi. Is this the person who told the fishing fleet about my sourdough?

SAOIRSE
It might be.

DEV (V.O.)
I'm Dev. I'm on the boat that took the bread.

SAOIRSE
The Marguerite?

DEV (V.O.)
The same.

SAOIRSE
They said the baker was an old man named Bernard.

DEV (V.O.)
Bernard is a rumor.

She laughs. She sets the kettle on its coil. Outside, a gull cries.

SAOIRSE
What do you want to hear, Dev?

DEV (V.O.)
Something slow. For the crew. We've been out three nights.

SAOIRSE
(to the room, a half-whisper)
Fine, ladies and gentlemen, one slow one for the Marguerite.

She flicks a switch. A low guitar begins. She cradles the phone against her shoulder.

SAOIRSE (CONT'D)
Is it going to be this easy every night?

DEV (V.O.)
If you call back.

SAOIRSE
I might.

DEV (V.O.)
Good. I'll bring the bread.

SAOIRSE
You'll be in the middle of the sea.

DEV (V.O.)
Then I'll swim.

She grins, helpless, and switches the mic back on.

SAOIRSE (CONT'D)
(into mic)
That was for Dev, and for everyone with salt on their hands.
